fact WorkItem(id: "WI-878", description: some(value: "CLI/resolver: an undefined functor whose SHORT NAME collides with a resolver scoping marker escapes the WI-754/WI-863 undefined-functor report — silent `no solutions` instead of a loud refusal (Case C of WI-863, measured 2026-07-30). The four reserved marker short names — `forall_in` / `some_in` (WI-027 bounded quantification), `forall_impl` (WI-108 hereditary-Harrop), `__pop_assumption` (scope discharge) — are exempted by `undefined_query_functor` (kb/mod.rs) via the shared authority `is_scoping_marker_name` (kb/resolve.rs), because the RESOLVER also keys these forms on the SHORT name (`bounded_quant_kind` -> `resolve_sym`) and reserves the whole name: even a qualified `myns.some_in` is intercepted as a malformed quantifier. So reporting one as 'undefined' while the resolver hijacks it would be INCOHERENT — the exemption is deliberate and consistent, not a CLI bug. RESIDUAL (the only harm): a user who genuinely typos one of these four names gets silent `no solutions` instead of the loud undefined-functor error every other typo gets. Measured: `anthill query -p kb 'some_in(x)'` with `some_in` declared nowhere -> `no solutions` (exit 0); a real `some_in` needs arity 3 (binder, collection, body), so `some_in/1` is a malformed quantifier the resolver treats as failure. LOW VALUE: 4 unusual names, `__pop_assumption` a double-underscore internal one never types by accident. Recorded here (per loud-error-over-silent-skip) so the boundary stays tracked after WI-863 is delivered; explicitly OUT of WI-863's acceptance. FIX DIRECTION (unsettled): the fix belongs in the RESOLVER, not the CLI — tighten marker recognition so a user name cannot collide: (a) ARITY-CHECK (a real forall_in/some_in/forall_impl is arity 3, __pop_assumption arity 1, so an off-arity call is NOT a marker and CAN be reported undefined), or (b) move the markers to a RESERVED NAMESPACE. LOAD-BEARING CONSTRAINT: the CLI exemption (`is_scoping_marker_name`) and the resolver's marker recognition are ONE shared authority and MUST be tightened IN LOCKSTEP — do NOT diverge the two independently, or a CLI that reports `some_in/1` while the resolver still hijacks the name would disagree with resolution. Soft-follows WI-863 (case C); no technical blocker (independently workable). Rust-only (resolver marker recognition + the shared name check). ACCEPTANCE: a genuine typo of a marker short name at a non-marker arity is either reported undefined (loud) or made impossible by arity/namespace tightening; a well-formed bounded-quantifier / forall_impl / __pop_assumption query still resolves unchanged; the CLI undefined-functor check and the resolver marker recognition remain a single shared authority (no divergence); a test drives the typo case and a well-formed-marker regression; full workspace green via rustland/scripts/test.sh."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: nil), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: Delivered(agent: "claude", at: "2026-07-31T07:18:35Z"))
